  "translation": {
    "literal": "The moon is of the Qin era, the pass is of the Han era; ten thousand li they march, and the men have not returned. If only the Flying General of Dragon City were still here, he would not let the barbarian horses cross the Yin Mountains.",
    "poetic": "The same moon shone in Qin times, the same pass in Han, yet the men who marched ten thousand miles have not come home. If only the Flying General of Dragon City lived, no foreign horseman would cross the Yin Mountains."
  },
  "notes": [
    {
      "id": "hu-wen",
      "kind": "interpretation",
      "title": "One moon, one pass, two dynasties",
      "body": "The first line is famous for a figure of speech: the moon and the pass belong to both the Qin and the Han. So the line means that the same moon and the same pass have stood for a thousand years, and the border war has lasted just as long. The Tang poet is writing about his own time through the old dynasties."
    },
    {
      "id": "general",
      "kind": "history",
      "title": "The Flying General",
      "body": "飛將 is Li Guang (died 119 BC), a Han general whom the Xiongnu are said to have feared. 龍城 was a Xiongnu ritual centre; some editions read 盧城 or 盧龍城 instead. The poem wishes for such a commander now, and, by implication, criticises the generals of the poet's own day."
    },
    {
      "id": "rank",
      "kind": "history",
      "title": "A famous judgement",
      "body": "Wang Changling (698–757) was called the \"master of the seven-character jueju\". A Ming critic is said to have named this the greatest of all Tang jueju. Whether or not that is true, it shows the poem's reputation."
    },
    {
      "id": "rhyme",
      "kind": "rhyme",
      "title": "Rhyme: 關 · 還 · 山",
      "body": "Lines one, two and four end in 關 (guān), 還 (huán) and 山 (shān), all in the 刪 rhyme. Line three ends in 在 and does not rhyme."
    }
  ],
